# nos-cc-skill

Claude Code plugin for Moxa NOS buildroot development — build, commit, GitLab workflow, codebase research, event log generation, BR2 feature-flag gating, and the **nos-sdd-\*** SDD document family (requirements / design / test specs, implementation orchestration, chain router).

## Prerequisites

- [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code) installed and working
- Git credentials (SSH key or HTTPS token) with access to this GitLab repo
- Python 3 with `pyyaml` module (required by `nos-workflow.py`)

## Install

Run these two commands inside a Claude Code session:

```
/plugin marketplace add git@gitlab.com:moxa/sw/switch/personal/miles.chen.switch/nos-cc-skill.git
/plugin install nos-cc-skill@nos-cc-skill
```

If SSH doesn't work, use HTTPS instead:

```
/plugin marketplace add https://gitlab.com/moxa/sw/switch/personal/miles.chen.switch/nos-cc-skill.git
/plugin install nos-cc-skill@nos-cc-skill
```

### Verify

After installing, restart Claude Code and run `/plugin`. You should see:

```
nos-cc-skill@nos-cc-skill
  Version: <current version>
  Scope: user
  Status: enabled
```

## Update

```
/plugin update nos-cc-skill
```

Claude Code also auto-updates plugins at startup. For background auto-updates on private repos (no interactive prompt), set a GitLab token in your shell profile:

```bash
# ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc
export GITLAB_TOKEN=glpat-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
```

The token needs at least `read_repository` scope.

## Uninstall

```
/plugin uninstall nos-cc-skill@nos-cc-skill
```

## Skills Included

### nos-build-buildroot

Build and verify buildroot packages via Docker containers. Detects the project environment, derives the container name, selects build strategy (rebuild/dirclean/clean), and handles Rust framework dependencies.

**Triggers automatically** when you finish code changes and want to verify compilation.

### nos-commit

Staging-verified commit workflow. Surveys changes, groups by area, confirms scope, stages explicitly, verifies staging, then generates a commit message with issue prefix auto-detection from the GitLab workflow manifest.

**Invoke with:** `/nos-commit`

### nos-gitlab-workflow

Orchestrates GitLab issues and MRs for multi-repo NOS changes. Three workflows:
- **Create** — new main issue + per-repo sub-issues + MRs
- **Add Repo** — add repos to an existing main issue
- **Update** — new MR after a previous one was merged

Uses a persistent manifest (`nos-workflow.py`) to track state across sessions.

#### Active-issue commands

The skill uses a HEAD-style active pointer (`_active.yaml`) so every command always
acts on the issue you explicitly selected. There is **no fallback guess** — if the
pointer is absent or broken, the command gates and lists open candidates instead of
picking the highest-numbered one silently.

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `$WF active` | Print the active issue number (exit 3 if unset/dangling, exit 6 if closed) |
| `$WF set-active <N>` | Set #N as the active issue; omit N to list candidates with `[active]` tag |
| `$WF close <N> [--force]` | Sync → verify no open MRs → close #N on GitLab → clear the active pointer |
| `$WF local-only` | Print repo IDs excluded from GitLab tracking (from `.claude/workflow/local-only.yaml`); the skill drops these from issue/MR scope silently |

**Triggers automatically** when you need to create GitLab issues/MRs for multi-repo changes.

### nos-research

Structured NOS/ISS codebase investigation with three modes:

- **Full research** — End-to-end feature investigation with 4 phases: scope entry points, trace paths with parallel agents, document with mermaid diagrams and gap analysis, interactive review. Produces a research doc.
- **Quick trace** — Single-path question or debug lookup. Inline answer with file paths and evidence, no doc.
- **Codebase + external** — Codebase trace first, then web search for vendor/industry comparison.

Identifies process boundaries (moxashell/framework/ISS), enforces skip rules for deprecated code. Ships an architecture navigation map ([`references/architecture.md`](skills/nos-research/references/architecture.md)) of NOS-generic source-tracing traps as four generating principles (build-selected live variant · config's persisted home · representation shifts across layers · ISS multi-threading).

**Invoke with:** `/nos-research <topic>` or triggers automatically during codebase investigation.

### nos-eventlog

Generate verified, accurate event log lists for NOS switch products. Uses a **tiered acceleration system** to classify events by verification cost:

- **Tier 1** — 30 always-supported events (zero verification)
- **Tier 2** — ISS module events determined by SW_PACKAGE (one check)
- **Tier 3** — Single-flag-gated groups (one `.config` flag per group)
- **Tier 4** — Unknown/new events only (full trigger-code verification)

Produces two documents:
- **Internal doc** — Event IDs, CEF data, source traceability, Not Supported section with reasons
- **Customer-facing doc** — Simplified table with severity and message

**Invoke with:** `/nos-eventlog` or triggers automatically when you mention event logs, syslog events, or CEF event IDs for NOS products.

### nos-repos

Multi-repo git operations across a NOS buildroot workspace (buildroot root + `dl/*` + moxa). One pure-bash script, no LLM. Subcommands:

- **status** — one cross-repo table (STATE, BRANCH, BASE, AHEAD, BEHIND, DIRTY, latest title). DIRTY shows staged/unstaged counts: `S (2)` / `U (47)` / `SU (2/47)`. Offline by default; AHEAD is computed locally, BEHIND only with `--fetch`/`--behind`. Attention filters `--dirty`/`--ahead`/`--behind` (OR-combined); `--porcelain` for tab-separated L2 input.
- **diff** — raw `git diff` / `git log` vs base for selected repos (`--dirty` for uncommitted, `--base <ref>` for commits vs base).
- **fetch** — update remote-tracking refs; non-zero exit on any failure.
- **rebase** — `--auto` (per-repo `.config` target) fetches first and aborts the whole run if any fetch fails; or explicit `rebase <onto>`. Skips dirty (unless `--autostash`) and in-progress repos; fail-fast on conflict unless `--continue-on-conflict`.
- **checkout** — switch all in-scope repos to `<branch>`, skipping dirty/in-progress; `NO-BRANCH` where the branch is absent.
- **target** — resolve a repo's target branch from `buildroot/.config` (`BR2_PACKAGE_<NAME>_REV`) or `develop` fallback.

Input is always a repo list: discovery default, `--scope all|dl|<csv>`, or positional IDs — never a manifest. Mutating commands print a plan and confirm unless `--yes` (which requires explicit `--scope`).

**Invoke with:** `bash ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/nos-repos/nos-repos.sh <subcommand>` from inside the workspace, or triggers automatically when you ask about repo status, what changed across repos, or rebasing all repos.

### nos-feature-flag

Gate a buildroot capability behind a `BR2_*` Kconfig flag so a product line can build it in or out with **zero behavior/install difference when off**. Distinguishes the two NOS gating layers (Buildroot package vs. Rust framework feature) and gives ordered checklists + copy-paste templates for:

- **Pattern A** — a fine-grained behavior diff inside an existing package (C `-D`/`#ifdef`, or the Rust cargo-feature forwarding chain `rust_moxa_build.mk` → `app_moxa_framework` → `plugins_moxa_framework` → plugin crate + dummy script).
- **Pattern B** — a brand-new optional package (B1 framework-integrated plugin, or B2 standalone daemon).

Includes the multi-package umbrella-flag variant (e.g. LDAP across several crates), a footguns list (`select` vs `depends on`, dead `Config.in`, `default n`, `savedefconfig`, dummy-package script), and both-flag-state verification.

**Triggers automatically** when adding or isolating a feature behind a BR2 flag.

**Which nos-sdd-\* to invoke**: vague or state-based ask ("start feature X", "where is X") → `nos-sdd-workflow`; explicit document ask ("review the SRS", "write the STS", "update the api contract") → that document's skill directly; implementation → `nos-sdd-develop`, only after the SDS (with Stages) and STS v1 exist.

### nos-interview

The single pre-contract **settlement interview** entry point for NOS skills — a self-contained three-arm interview (vocabulary term sheet / falsifiable map laydown + human extraction / territory) specialized for the NOS buildroot domain. It keeps an AI-side laydown (not questioning-only), renders a `## Consensus` section, publishes consequence probes, and terminates at a readiness ruling.

- **NOS base-domain layer (always on)** — pre-seeds a small NOS-buildroot term sheet, supplies a base verdict vocabulary (`settled` / `needs-workshop` / `blocked-by-syrs`), and realizes the territory arm as a research subagent dispatch (carrying nos-research's rules + the grounding/citation discipline) so repo facts are looked up, never asked of the human.
- **Optional domain pack (four slots, each independently optional)** — a caller customizes the interview without forking it: question bank (slot 1) + priority order (slot 2) inject into extraction, verdict vocabulary (slot 3) overrides the base set, and a record sub-section (slot 4) **appends** after the fixed record skeleton. A slot that tries to overwrite/reorder a fixed section, or a malformed slot, is rejected with a surfaced error — never silently ignored.
- **Output is a confirmed-facts source** — the record's `## Consensus` (with per-fact `[trace: …]`) + `## Readiness ruling` satisfy the shared confirmed-facts-source contract, so a downstream contract author derives facts without re-eliciting them.

Chain-called by `nos-sdd-req`'s settlement loop; dependencies are one-way (`nos-sdd-req → nos-interview → nos-research`, no cycle).

**Invoke with:** `/nos-interview` or triggers on "settle this", "settlement loop", "interview before spec", "run the settlement interview".

### nos-sdd-req

Spec-time **generate / revise / review** for NOS requirement documents — **SyRS and SRS** (SAS/SDS/STS are a future sibling skill). Not an implementation-coverage check (that's the STS's job). Generation and revision are review-driven: interview / apply decisions → draft or edit (with cascade checklist: renumbering → cross-refs, renames → all surfaces, History rows, downstream sync) → self-review with the same checks → deliver.

- **SyRS review** — 9 commitment-quality checks (decided, right scope, testable, stated once, market-grounded — full list in `SKILL.md`). Output: Blocking gaps / Decisions needed / Non-blocking cleanup; verdict `settled` / `settle-after-answers` / `needs-workshop`.
- **Settlement loop** — SyRS generation, and the escalation when a SyRS is absent or unsettled, **delegate to `nos-interview`** (the shared settlement interview). nos-sdd-req supplies its SDD domain pack (the settlement question bank + gap-question priority order + `DECISION`/`ASSUMPTION` clarification-record format from `references/review-examples.md`) and consumes the interview's consensus + verdict, keeping only its SDD-specific concerns: SyRS-first ordering, and the tracked-assumption output (user-answered decisions vs engineering assumptions, never silent guesses).
- **SRS review** — gated on a settled SyRS — review degrades to a limited pre-review capped at `blocked-by-syrs`; generation is refused (or delivered only as a marked provisional draft). Nine ordered passes (0–8): template completeness (sections exist even if N/A, and N/A must be true) → platform-default pruning → single-home + conciseness → section roles → numbering/traceability/commitment → terminology → value-space honesty → testability → sibling alignment + genuine gaps. Deletion-biased with anti-inflation guards. Output: Delete/Consolidate / Correctness/Ambiguity / Add; objective verdict mapping (`ready` / `ready-after-deletes` / `needs-revision` / `blocked-by-syrs`).

Findings map to the five review dimensions (consistency / conciseness / correctness / relatedness / readability) with a per-dimension coverage note in the verdict. Worked examples and the settlement question bank are in `references/review-examples.md`.

**Invoke with:** `/nos-sdd-req` or triggers on "review syrs/srs", "write syrs/srs", "revise/update syrs/srs", "requirements review".

### nos-sdd-design

Generate / revise / review **SAS and SDS** (gated on a settled SRS, else `blocked-by-srs`). SAS is optional by default — written only for genuinely architectural features; skipped means the SDS opens with a brief Architecture Overview. Core checks: WHAT→WHERE→HOW level discipline (no SAS/SDS duplication — every fact has one home), **no code-recoverable detail in the SDS** (code is the source of truth; keep contracts, constraint/classification tables, rationale), **`## Implementation Stages` mandatory** (Scope / Done when / Depends on — no separate implementation-plan doc), reuse-first, open items code-gated, no spec IDs destined for code. Same three-bucket output and verdict mapping as nos-sdd-req.

**Invoke with:** `/nos-sdd-design` or triggers on "review sas/sds", "write/generate sds", "revise sds".

### nos-sdd-webapi

Generate / revise / review the **frontend-facing OpenAPI contract** (BFF for the UI team), gated on a settled SRS but **deliberately not on the SDS** — the contract exists so frontend and backend design run in parallel; design-dependent semantics carry `needs decision` markers resolved when the SDS lands. Core checks: BFF-not-a-mirror (never a 1:1 copy of the config schema), SRS vocabulary and value-space fidelity, guideline rules the linter can't see, pattern reuse from the repo's reference feature, mounted + lint-clean (`api-lint` 0 errors) before delivery.

**Invoke with:** `/nos-sdd-webapi` or triggers on "review web api", "write web api spec", "openapi contract".

### nos-sdd-test

Generate / revise / review the **STS** (gated on a settled SRS). The two signature checks: **every test command is copied from the SRS CLI section — never invented** — and the **reverse audit**: every SRS CLI command has a case (a real audit caught a command no test could reach). Plus: full REQ coverage that never shrinks, `Covers:` headers (no orphan tests), component/server boundary, write-then-readback and the other test principles, device-recoverability (restoration in finally), minimal-but-sufficient case sets, fixtures single-homed in the Appendix. Document skill only — running tests belongs to nos-sdd-develop.

**Invoke with:** `/nos-sdd-test` or triggers on "review sts", "write/generate sts", "test spec".

### nos-sdd-develop

Execution orchestrator — consumes the settled chain, produces code; **edits no spec content itself**. Entry gate = SDS with Implementation Stages + STS v1 full coverage. Per-stage loop: tests first → implement → buildroot build → STS scenarios green **on the real target** (full image → device upgrade → harness; package-build green is not done) → user-confirmed commit through the per-commit review gate → stage marked DONE via delegation. The core discipline is the **divergence protocol**: reality disagrees with a doc → stop coding → delegate the revision to nos-sdd-design / nos-sdd-test / nos-sdd-req → resume. Write/review context isolation for tests; code states considerations, never spec IDs.

**Invoke with:** `/nos-sdd-develop` or triggers on "implement feature", "next stage", "continue implementation".

### nos-sdd-workflow

Thin router at the top of the family for vague or state-based requests ("start feature X", "where is X now"). Scans the feature's chain state (SyRS link state, SRS/SAS/SDS/STS files, stage DONE markers), **announces the detected state and waits for confirmation — never auto-starts**, then dispatches to the right member. Holds the development gate (no implementation before SDS + STS v1). Pure dispatcher — no document or implementation logic; explicit single-station requests bypass it.

**Invoke with:** `/nos-sdd-workflow` or triggers on "start feature", "resume feature", "what's next for <feature>".
